      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. checked resource usage and node metrics—everything looks well within limits. cpu/mem usage is stable. \n\ni'm looking at the pod description now and it says the last termination reason was an OOMKilled, but when i check the metrics, the pod is nowhere near its limit before it dies. it's almost like it's being terminated by something external or a sudden spike we aren't catching. \n\ncan u help me figure out if there's any way to catch these sudden spikes or if there's something else in the GKE/K8s stack that would trigger an OOMKilled status even if the pod seems under the limit?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The OOMKilled termination reason can be misleading, especially when the pod's resource utilization appears to be within limits. To further investigate, I recommend the following:\n\n1. **Container Memory Allocations**: Investigate the container's memory allocation patterns using `kubectl top` or `kubectl describe`. Look for any sudden spikes in memory allocation, which could indicate a memory leak or a temporary increase in memory usage.\n2. **GKE's Memory Pressure Monitoring**: Enable GKE's memory pressure monitoring by setting the `--enable-memory-pressure-monitoring` flag when creating the cluster or updating the cluster configuration. This will provide more detailed information on memory pressure events, which may help identify the cause of the OOMKilled termination.\n3. **Kubernetes' Memory Pressure Events**: Check the Kubernetes event logs for memory pressure events using `kubectl logs -f <pod-name> -v=5`. This will provide more detailed information on the events that led to the OOMKilled termination.\n4. **Container's Memory Usage Over Time**: Use `kubectl top` or `kubectl describe` to monitor the container's memory usage over time. This can help identify any patterns or anomalies in memory usage that may indicate a memory leak or other issues.",
